{
  "title": "Where are you going?",
  "title_target": "Nereye gidiyorsun?",
  "goal": "Express direction with *-e* and origin with *-den*, then use both to get around town.",
  "bekal": [
    "tell apart *evde* (at home), *eve* (to the house) and *evden* (from the house)",
    "say how you travel with *ile* or its short form *-le*",
    "read signs and ask the way at bus stops, ferry piers and metro stations"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Dialogue — Vapurda",
      "intro": "Budi and Emre take the ferry from Kadıköy to Eminönü. The wind is strong and seagulls are chasing the boat.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Emre:",
          "text": "Bak, İstanbul'a böyle bakmak lazım.",
          "id": "Look, this is how you have to see Istanbul.",
          "literal": "look, Istanbul-to like-this to-look necessary."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "İnanılmaz. Bu vapur nereye gidiyor?",
          "id": "Unbelievable. Where is this ferry going?",
          "literal": "unbelievable. this ferry where-to is-going?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Emre:",
          "text": "Eminönü'ye. Kadıköy'den Eminönü'ye yirmi dakika.",
          "id": "To Eminönü. Kadıköy to Eminönü is twenty minutes.",
          "literal": "Eminönü-to. Kadıköy-from Eminönü-to twenty minute."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Sonra nereye gidiyoruz?",
          "id": "Where are we going after that?",
          "literal": "after where-to are-going-we?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Emre:",
          "text": "Kapalıçarşı'ya. Tramvayla gidiyoruz.",
          "id": "To the Grand Bazaar. We're taking the tram.",
          "literal": "Covered-Bazaar-to. tram-with are-going-we."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Tramvay nereden kalkıyor?",
          "id": "Where does the tram leave from?",
          "literal": "tram where-from is-departing?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Emre:",
          "text": "İskeleden. Vapurdan iniyoruz, hemen karşısında.",
          "id": "From the pier. We get off the ferry, and it's right across.",
          "literal": "pier-from. ferry-from are-getting-off-we, right opposite-its-at."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Bilet nereden alıyoruz?",
          "id": "Where do we buy tickets?",
          "literal": "ticket where-from are-taking-we?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Emre:",
          "text": "Bilet yok, İstanbulkart var. Sana bir tane alıyorum.",
          "id": "There are no tickets, there's the İstanbulkart. I'll get you one.",
          "literal": "ticket there-isn't, Istanbul-card there-is. you-to one piece am-taking-I."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Çok teşekkür ederim. Sana borcum var.",
          "id": "Thanks so much. I owe you.",
          "literal": "very thanks do-I. you-to debt-my there-is."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Emre:",
          "text": "Boş ver. Sen bana çay ısmarlıyorsun, tamam mı?",
          "id": "Forget it. You're buying me a tea, OK?",
          "literal": "empty give. you me-to tea are-treating-you, OK question?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Anlaştık!",
          "id": "Deal!",
          "literal": "agreed-we!"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Three endings, one place.** *Ev-de* = at home. *Ev-e* = to the house. *Ev-den* = from the house. All three attach to the same word, and Turkish has no prepositions at all — every question of direction is settled at the tail of the word.",
        "**Gitmek always requires -e.** \"Go to Ankara\" is *Ankara'ya gitmek*, never *Ankara gitmek*. The same goes for *binmek* (to get on a vehicle): *otobüse binmek*, literally \"get on TO the bus\". By contrast *inmek* (to get off) requires *-den*: *otobüsten inmek*.",
        "**Boş ver literally means \"give empty\"** — that is, \"never mind, forget it\". One of the few expressions you can't guess from its words, and one of the ones you'll hear most.",
        "**Anlaştık** means \"we've agreed\", from *anlaşmak* (to understand each other). It's in the past because the deal has just been made — Turkish treats it as done the moment the word is spoken, much like English \"Done!\"."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The ending -e: to, toward, for",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The direction ending has just two forms — *-e* and *-a* — plus a linking **y** if the word ends in a vowel. Hard consonants don't matter here, because the ending doesn't start with *d*."
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Word",
            "+ direction",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "ev",
              "ev**e**",
              "to the house, home"
            ],
            [
              "okul",
              "okul**a**",
              "to school"
            ],
            [
              "İstanbul",
              "İstanbul'**a**",
              "to Istanbul"
            ],
            [
              "Ankara",
              "Ankara'**ya**",
              "to Ankara"
            ],
            [
              "sinema",
              "sinema**ya**",
              "to the movies"
            ],
            [
              "sokak",
              "soka**ğa**",
              "to the street (k softens)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Personal pronouns change shape a little and must be memorized: **bana** (to me), **sana** (to you), **ona**, **bize**, **size**, **onlara**. The first two break the pattern — not *bene* and *sene*."
        },
        {
          "type": "sub",
          "text": "Verbs that require -e"
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Türkçe",
            "English",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "gitmek",
              "to go to",
              "Okula gidiyorum."
            ],
            [
              "gelmek",
              "to come to",
              "Bize geliyor musun?"
            ],
            [
              "binmek",
              "to get on",
              "Otobüse biniyoruz."
            ],
            [
              "bakmak",
              "to look at, to look after",
              "Denize bakıyorum."
            ],
            [
              "sormak",
              "to ask (someone)",
              "Sana bir şey soruyorum."
            ],
            [
              "başlamak",
              "to start (something)",
              "Derse başlıyoruz."
            ],
            [
              "yardım etmek",
              "to help",
              "Bana yardım eder misin?"
            ],
            [
              "inanmak",
              "to believe",
              "Sana inanıyorum."
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The ending -den: from, since, because of",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The origin ending has **four forms**, because it starts with *d* and obeys Fıstıkçı Şahap: *-den*, *-dan*, *-ten*, *-tan*."
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Word",
            "+ origin",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "ev",
              "ev**den**",
              "from the house"
            ],
            [
              "okul",
              "okul**dan**",
              "from school"
            ],
            [
              "iş",
              "iş**ten**",
              "from work"
            ],
            [
              "uçak",
              "uçak**tan**",
              "from the plane"
            ],
            [
              "Jakarta",
              "Jakarta'**dan**",
              "from Jakarta"
            ],
            [
              "ben / sen",
              "ben**den** / sen**den**",
              "from me / from you"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Besides direction, *-den* also expresses material (*tahtadan* — made of wood), cause (*yorgunluktan* — from tiredness), and comparison (*benden büyük* — bigger than me, see Unit 16)."
        },
        {
          "type": "sub",
          "text": "From where to where"
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Türkçe",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Nereden geliyorsun?",
              "Where are you coming from?"
            ],
            [
              "Nereye gidiyorsun?",
              "Where are you going?"
            ],
            [
              "Evden okula gidiyorum.",
              "I go from home to school."
            ],
            [
              "Kadıköy'den Eminönü'ye.",
              "From Kadıköy to Eminönü."
            ],
            [
              "Saat dokuzdan üçe kadar.",
              "From nine to three."
            ],
            [
              "Buradan uzak mı?",
              "Is it far from here?"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Nereli, nereden, nerede",
          "items": [
            "*Nerelisin?* asks about your origins — the city or country you come from. The answer is *Endonezyalıyım*.",
            "*Nereden geliyorsun?* asks where you've just come from. The answer is *okuldan*, *işten*, *evden*.",
            "*Neredesin?* asks where you are right now. Three questions, three endings, one and the same root: *nere*."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "How you get there",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The word *ile* means \"with\" and \"and\". In conversation it almost always attaches as **-le / -la**, with a linking *y* after a vowel."
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Long form",
            "Attached form",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "otobüs ile",
              "otobüs**le**",
              "by bus"
            ],
            [
              "araba ile",
              "araba**yla**",
              "by car"
            ],
            [
              "Ayşe ile",
              "Ayşe'**yle**",
              "with Ayşe"
            ],
            [
              "ben ile",
              "**benimle**",
              "with me"
            ],
            [
              "sen ile",
              "**seninle**",
              "with you"
            ],
            [
              "o ile",
              "**onunla**",
              "with him/her"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Türkçe",
            "English",
            "Türkçe",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
